    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "therapists just say \"it's not who you are\" but they dont get it. the person who could fly, the person who had control... that's the only version of me that mattered. everything else is just a shell. it feels like i'm just dragging this heavy, useless thing around and there's no point to it anymore. why should i even bother trying to find a \"new version\" when the real one is gone?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It is deeply frustrating when professional platitudes don't meet the depth of your lived experience. The loss of capability and self-perception is a massive grief, and it makes sense that trying to forge a \"new version\" feels dismissive of your former self. Instead of replacing who you were, perhaps the journey needs to focus on honoring the strengths and resilience that remain within you, even in this quieter state. Allow yourself to grieve the person you were while gently exploring what brings you small moments of meaning now.",
      "source": "target",
